WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.379 --> 00:00:02.487
(bird chirps)

00:00:02.487 --> 00:00:04.168
(bird crows)

00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:06.987
- [Narrator] You know this
guy, William Shakespeare.

00:00:06.987 --> 00:00:09.579
He's widely regarded
as the greatest writer

00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:11.237
in the English language.

00:00:11.237 --> 00:00:13.712
And here's Steve with
his Harris' hawk Margo.

00:00:13.712 --> 00:00:14.545
Hi Steve.

00:00:15.497 --> 00:00:17.328
What do they both have in common?

00:00:17.328 --> 00:00:18.578
Let me explain.

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:21.607
So, what is falconry?

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:24.547
- Falconry is defined
as the art of using a

00:00:24.547 --> 00:00:27.508
trained bird of prey flying at quarry.

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:30.417
Essentially it's catching your
dinner using a bird of prey.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:32.327
- [Narrator] It's been
around for thousands of years

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:34.838
and it was so popular
back in the 16th century

00:00:34.838 --> 00:00:38.022
that Shakespeare starting
using it in his literature.

00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:40.011
He was such a prolific falconer

00:00:40.011 --> 00:00:42.359
that everyone started using his phrases.

00:00:42.359 --> 00:00:44.491
So much so, these
expressions are ingrained

00:00:44.491 --> 00:00:46.741
in the English language today.

00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:48.137
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:00:48.137 --> 00:00:49.498
Like fed up.

00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:51.748
- When Margo has been out
hunting, what happens is

00:00:51.748 --> 00:00:53.721
she will eat until she
gets to a point where

00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:56.180
literally she is fed up
to here, she is full.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.030
She doesn't want to do
anything so she sits still,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.230
looks miserable, and
therefore we say to people

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.889
like the kids, "Look at you
sitting there looking fed up."

00:01:02.889 --> 00:01:04.502
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:07.761
(typewriter clicks)

00:01:07.761 --> 00:01:10.060
All birds, for thousands
of years, have always worn

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.932
these two little jesses,
these leather straps,

00:01:11.932 --> 00:01:14.153
attached to each leg,
and when we hold onto her

00:01:14.153 --> 00:01:16.662
we put them under the thumb
and through the fingers.

00:01:16.662 --> 00:01:19.252
Therefore we have restraint,
therefore we can hold him back

00:01:19.252 --> 00:01:21.102
just like an errant husband.

00:01:21.102 --> 00:01:22.241
All you've got to bear in mind is that

00:01:22.241 --> 00:01:24.332
if something's been around for so long,

00:01:24.332 --> 00:01:26.620
then it's bound to have an
impact on the vernacular.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.159
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:30.188
(bird crows)

00:01:30.188 --> 00:01:32.417
The falcon generally wears
a hood because they're very

00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:34.297
highly strung, very
nervous, and the hood is a

00:01:34.297 --> 00:01:35.255
calming down device.

00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:36.746
What we do is when we put the hood on him,

00:01:36.746 --> 00:01:38.987
we're actually fooling him
into thinking it's gone dark.

00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:41.537
So you've tricked him, I
hoodwinked him, using a hood

00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:43.605
to think it's nighttime so he relaxes.

00:01:43.605 --> 00:01:47.188
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:01:49.765 --> 00:01:51.653
As well as holding these
leather straps or jesses,

00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:53.794
the leash fastens onto the glove and then

00:01:53.794 --> 00:01:55.966
we wrap it around a finger,
it's like a belt and braces,

00:01:55.966 --> 00:01:58.515
it's an extra precaution
in case we lose the bird.

00:01:58.515 --> 00:02:02.098
(triumphant trumpet music)

00:02:03.316 --> 00:02:05.156
(bird crows)

00:02:05.156 --> 00:02:09.334
Haggard comes from an old
bird that has learned to hunt.

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:10.951
You trap her  once she's learned to hunt,

00:02:10.951 --> 00:02:12.422
therefore she's a better hunter because

00:02:12.422 --> 00:02:14.193
she's learned on her own,
which is where we get the word

00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:15.654
an old hag from now.

00:02:15.654 --> 00:02:17.393
How many people have used that every day

00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:20.740
and don't realize it's
actually a falconry expression?

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.932
- [Narrator] So there we have it.

00:02:21.932 --> 00:02:23.682
Thanks, Shakespeare.

